<header class="guide-hero wrap"><p class="eyebrow">Documentation</p><h1>A small form.<br>A clear contract.</h1><p class="lead">Start with the local setup guide, then use these references to understand what your form sends and what access you grant.</p><a class="button" href="/getting-started">Start the local guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></header>
<div class="wrap guide-layout"><nav class="guide-nav" aria-label="Documentation topics"><strong>On this page</strong><a href="#schema">Schema and integration</a><a href="#origins">Sites and origins</a><a href="#versions">Versions and retries</a><a href="#permissions">Permissions</a><a href="#publication">Publication</a><a href="#inbox">The inbox</a><a href="#troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</a><a href="#references">Versioned references</a></nav><div class="guide-body">
<section id="schema"><h2>Schema and form integration</h2><p>A schema defines the data your form accepts. GoFormX uses JSON Schema Draft 2020-12. For a contact form, require name, email and message, set useful bounds and reject unexpected fields.</p><pre tabindex="0" aria-label="Example contact draft specification"><code>{
  "name": "site-a-contact",
  "title": "Contact",
  "siteId": "RETURNED_SITE_ID",
  "allowedOrigins": ["https://site-a.example"],
  "schema": {
    "$schema": "https://json-schema.org/draft/2020-12/schema",
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
      "name": {"type": "string", "minLength": 1, "maxLength": 100},
      "email": {"type": "string", "format": "email", "maxLength": 254},
      "message": {"type": "string", "minLength": 10, "maxLength": 5000}
    },
    "required": ["name", "email", "message"],
    "additionalProperties": false
  }
}</code></pre><p>This is a specification example, not a published form. Your frontend retains its own markup and styling. The public submission request wraps field values in <code>{"data": {…}}</code>; it does not send management credentials.</p><p>Fetch the public schema at <code>GET /v1/public/forms/{publicKey}/schema</code>. Submit to <code>POST /v1/public/forms/{publicKey}/submissions</code>. See the <a href="#references">canonical OpenAPI reference</a> for the full contract.</p></section>
<section id="origins"><h2>Sites and allowed origins</h2><p>A site identifies the browser origin where a form belongs. Organizations own resources; organization IDs do not stand in for site IDs.</p><p>Store the browser-serialized origin exactly, including scheme and any non-default port. An origin has no path: <code>https://site-a.example</code>, not <code>https://site-a.example/contact</code>. The generated module checks the page origin, and the API checks the request's raw Origin header.</p><p>The local client verifies CORS preflight and the submission response. A generated module alone does not prove that the browser can submit.</p></section>
<section id="versions"><h2>Versions and retries</h2><p>Accepted submissions retain their immutable schema version. Read <code>X-GoFormX-Schema-Version</code> from the schema response and send that version with the public submission.</p><p>Use a stable <code>Idempotency-Key</code> for each logical submission. If the response is lost, retain the user's inputs and retry with the same payload and key. Do not silently replace the key or create another form after an uncertain outcome.</p><p>A public accepted response is HTTP 202. Confirm its submission ID, accepted status and version. A same-key retry may report <code>Idempotency-Replayed: true</code>. Acceptance is not proof of downstream notification delivery.</p></section>
<section id="permissions"><h2>Give the assistant only the access it needs</h2><p>The default draft connection uses <code>forms:read</code> and <code>forms:write</code>. Publication requires a deliberate <code>forms:publish</code> grant. Reading people's messages requires the separate <code>submissions:read</code> permission and an explicit privacy decision.</p><p>Issue credentials through the authorized owner/admin dashboard flow. Keep them in local or server-side secret custody, outside repositories and browser bundles. GoFormX grants enforce access; an assistant's prompt is not an authorization boundary.</p><p>Revoke a connection when setup is complete or access is no longer appropriate. Do not log one-time token reveals or recover credentials from metadata.</p></section>
<section id="publication"><h2>Publication is a human decision</h2><p>Creating a draft and generating code do not publish a form. Review the schema, origin and site; approve the exact form and version. The client publication command requires <code>--confirm</code> and the matching scope.</p><p>The current public collection endpoint accepts only published schemas. A failed synthetic check is a failure to qualify the integration, not evidence that publication did not occur.</p></section>
<section id="inbox"><h2>Your inbox is the submissions dashboard</h2><p>Sign in and open <strong>All submissions</strong>. Owners and admins can view authorized responses across sites, filter by site or form, and inspect the exact accepted schema. Members do not receive access to submission content.</p><p>The control plane reads through Go's authorized API; it does not keep another submission store. Inbox receipt is separate from email. Email notifications are not yet verified.</p><a class="text-link" href="/login">Sign in to your workspace <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></section>
<section id="troubleshooting"><h2>When a step fails</h2><ul><li><strong>401:</strong> the connection needs reauthorization. Check expiry or revocation.</li><li><strong>403:</strong> check the deliberate grant and the acting user's organization role. Do not broaden access automatically.</li><li><strong>Origin/CORS failure:</strong> compare the site's exact origin, allowed origin and browser headers.</li><li><strong>Validation failure:</strong> read the path-addressable field errors against the published schema.</li><li><strong>Lost response:</strong> preserve intent state, payload and idempotency key. Reconcile before issuing another mutation.</li><li><strong>No email:</strong> use the authorized inbox receipt. Email notifications and emailed account recovery remain unavailable until mail is configured and qualified.</li></ul><p>Keep synthetic tests in your own controlled environment. Do not send tests to the live working example.</p></section>
